WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280
Choosing the problem.

2
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:04.120
Good capstone problems share four properties.

3
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.957
A named user with a job.

4
00:00:06.237 --> 00:00:13.043
Not “educators” but “an instructional designer checking a course page against WCAG before handoff.

5
00:00:13.323 --> 00:00:22.688
” Google’s problem-framing course asks you to state the ideal outcome independently of any model: what the product should do for the user, before you think about how.

6
00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:28.024
If you can’t describe the outcome without saying “AI,” you haven’t found the problem yet.

7
00:00:28.304 --> 00:00:30.949
A current process you can describe with a number.

8
00:00:31.229 --> 00:00:34.408
How long does it take today, and how often does it go wrong?

9
00:00:34.688 --> 00:00:42.667
Your own experience in course development and delivery is a legitimate source here, as long as you describe patterns rather than clients.

10
00:00:42.947 --> 00:00:49.603
The number doesn’t have to be precise, but it anchors your success metric and makes the write-up land with non-engineers.

11
00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:51.163
Checkable outputs.

12
00:00:51.443 --> 00:01:02.771
Retrieval, extraction, compliance, and status questions have answers you can label: the requirement is in the document or it isn’t, the citation supports the claim or it doesn’t.

13
00:01:03.051 --> 00:01:08.981
Open-ended generation (“write a better course description”) is much harder to evaluate in six weeks.

14
00:01:09.261 --> 00:01:15.789
If you can’t imagine writing 100 test cases with expected outcomes, narrow the problem until you can.

15
00:01:16.069 --> 00:01:18.075
Public or synthetic data only.

16
00:01:18.355 --> 00:01:27.549
The repo is public, so client documents, learner records, contracts, and internal trackers are off the table, including in screenshots and traces.

17
00:01:27.829 --> 00:01:35.851
Public standards, open courseware, government RFPs, and synthetic corpora you generate (and label as synthetic) all work.

18
00:01:36.131 --> 00:01:38.349
Check each source’s license and record it.

19
00:01:38.629 --> 00:01:41.851
Finally, choose the simplest architecture that could work.

20
00:01:42.131 --> 00:01:51.432
Anthropic’s guidance on effective agents recommends starting with the simplest solution and adding agentic autonomy only when it demonstrably helps.

21
00:01:51.712 --> 00:02:01.227
The capstone requires an agent with tools, so find the part of the job that genuinely needs judgment about what to do next, and keep the rest as a fixed workflow.
